<FeaturePage
  tagline="FEATURE // OBSERVATION_SUBSTRATE"
  title="Kernel state as a filesystem"
  description="Every fact the kernel knows projects as a path. ls and cat work. No JSON parser, no helper tool — just files. The kernel is your VFS."
  relatedPages={relatedPages}
>
  <h2>The kernel is mounted</h2>
  <p>
    Run <code>ostk vfs mount</code>. A FUSE filesystem appears at <code>.ostk/vfs/</code>. Inside, the kernel's live state shows up as ordinary directories and files: <code>needles/</code>, <code>decisions/</code>, <code>fleet/</code>, <code>journal/</code>, <code>sys/</code>, <code>mem/</code>, <code>proc/</code>, <code>drivers/</code>. Standard tools work — <code>ls</code>, <code>cat</code>, <code>tree</code>, <code>find</code>, <code>grep</code>. The daemon is the backing process; reading a path consults the live registry.
  </p>

  <h2>Three projection tiers</h2>
  <p>
    The same fact projects at three resolutions, ratified as the path-projection lattice doctrine. <strong>Atom</strong>: <code>cat /ostk/needles/1338/title</code> returns one line — 1 to 30 tokens. <strong>Object</strong>: reading the parent directory or an <code>_object</code> sentinel returns the full row. <strong>Namespace</strong>: <code>ls /ostk/needles/</code> enumerates every needle in the project. Agents and operators pick the resolution they need; the daemon serves the smallest answer that satisfies the read.
  </p>

  <h2>Why this matters for agents</h2>
  <p>
    An agent that wants the title of needle 1338 doesn't call a tool, parse JSON, or invoke a helper command. It runs <code>cat .ostk/vfs/needles/1338/title</code>. The model already knows how to read files. The kernel becomes legible through the same primitive every Unix tool already uses, and the read budget collapses to a few tokens instead of a whole record.
  </p>

  <h2>Live state, not snapshots</h2>
  <p>
    The VFS is not a dump. It's a live view onto the daemon's registry. Cache hit counters, fleet membership, needle state, decision rows — all reflect the running system at the moment of read. Audit-row events flow through a Postgres-logical-replication-style subscriber dispatcher, the same one that maintains the searchable graph index. The VFS is one consumer of that substrate.
  </p>

  <h2>Build flag and lifecycle</h2>
  <p>
    The mount requires <code>--features fuse</code> at build time and macFUSE or libfuse on the host. <code>ostk vfs status</code> reports declared vs. actual mount points and the owning daemon's pid. <code>ostk vfs unmount-stale</code> idempotently reaps orphan macfuse entries left by SIGKILL'd daemons. Source: <code>src/kernel/vfs/</code>.
  </p>
</FeaturePage>
